DwarfSpec Source Organization Implementation
============================================

Purpose:
    ☐ Implement `docs/source-organization-proposal.md` without changing public DwarfSpec behavior.
    ☐ Organize production code by execution boundary and role: controller, host, driver, protocol, and support.
    ☐ Preserve the shallow `dwarfspec.cli`, `dwarfspec.layout`, and `dwarfspec.ds` root facades.
    ☐ Mirror organized production namespaces beneath `tests/unit/`.
    ☐ Decompose the six approved large-module hotspots only after the initial directory migration is complete.

Completion contract:
    ☐ Treat this checklist as the implementation source of truth and `docs/source-organization-proposal.md` as the authority for architectural intent and the initial Mermaid tree.
    ☐ Complete every applicable implementation, test, documentation, cleanup, and evidence item below.
    ☐ Stop and reconcile the proposal and this checklist before proceeding if repository facts invalidate a target ownership decision or dependency rule.
    ☐ Preserve the public `dwarfspec` executable behavior and the injected run-scoped `ds` API.
    ☐ Treat every Lua source module path as internal; move internal modules atomically and do not add forwarding shims at former paths.
    ☐ Keep `src/ds.d.lua` at the source root and preserve its declaration behavior.
    ☐ Preserve deliberate `loadfile()` freshness and cache behavior in each composition root; do not replace it with ordinary `require()` as part of this work.
    ☐ Keep loading helpers local to `ds.lua` and the host composition root; do not introduce a shared `module_loader` system.
    ☐ Do not introduce dependency-auditing machinery that replaces, wraps, intercepts, or overrides `require()`, `loadfile()`, `dofile()`, package searchers, or module environments.
    ☐ Do not add tests whose only purpose is to assert that particular production files exist.
    ☐ Do not add packaged-source or archive verification; the source checkout is the verification target for this reorganization.
    ☐ Validate declarations from the source checkout only; do not use an installed rock or packaged declaration tree for this gate.
    ☐ Do not combine this work with public API changes, TestBed implementation, test-runner UI behavior, or selector redesign.
    ☐ Add LuaDoc-style prose for every new or materially extracted Lua module and function in every phase.
    ☐ Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not stash, discard, or commit user changes.
    ☐ Use `git mv` for tracked moves and keep directory moves, behavior fixes, and decompositions as separate reviewable changesets; do not create commits unless explicitly requested.

Standard phase gate:
    ☐ Run the affected focused tests.
    ☐ Run the full recursive source-checkout unit suite.
    ☐ Run `tools/Check-Lua.ps1`.
    ☐ Run `git diff --check`.
    ☐ If the phase changed host entry paths, dynamic loading, or live execution behavior, run the affected focused live tests and report test success and `cleanup_confirmed` separately.
    ☐ Do not rerun the full non-destructive live suite here; after Phase 0, that sweep is reserved for the two milestone phases and is compared with the Phase 0 baseline.

Phase 0 - Initial test baseline:
    ☑ Record the active checkout, branch, worktree state, and index state without modifying them.
    ☑ Record the current production-module and unit-spec inventory needed to detect omissions during moves.
    ☑ Inventory static `require()` calls, literal and constructed `loadfile()` targets, and host entry-script paths returned by `dwarfspec.layout`.
    ☑ Run and record the full source-checkout unit suite with its executed test count.
    ☑ Run and record `tools/Check-Lua.ps1`.
    ☑ Establish one reproducible source-declaration check using `tests/declarations/source.luarc.json` before production files move.
        ☑ Require `tests/declarations/valid/` to produce no unexpected LuaLS diagnostics.
        ☑ Require `tests/declarations/invalid/` to produce the expected bounded diagnostic set so the check detects declarations that become missing or too permissive.
        ☑ Record the LuaLS version, invocation, and baseline results without loading an installed or packaged DwarfSpec tree.
    ☑ Run and record focused live baselines for component mounting, native-screen mounting, pointer input, events, save-game commands, cleanup, and scheduler recovery.
    ☑ Run and record the full non-destructive live suite.
    ☑ Run and record `tools/Run-MultiProjectIntegration.ps1` as the multi-process integration baseline.
    ☑ Record every pre-existing live or multi-process integration failure separately so later gates can distinguish regressions from baseline failures.
    ☑ Record `cleanup_confirmed` separately from test success for every live baseline.
    ☑ Record the completed baseline and known failures in `docs/source-organization-phase0-baseline.md`.
    ☑ Do not begin source moves until the baseline evidence is complete.

Phase 1 - Establish recursive discovery and path authority:
    ☑ Remove Busted's `--no-recursive` argument from `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` and the repository CI unit-test invocation.
    ☑ Search the remaining test tooling for Busted invocations and ensure none disables recursive discovery for `tests/unit/`.
    ☑ Extend `dwarfspec.layout` as the single authority for source and installed paths to host entry scripts.
    ☑ Replace duplicated host entry-script path literals in controller callers and tests with the layout authority.
    ☑ Keep small local loading functions in `ds.lua` and the host composition root.
    ☑ Mechanically derive source paths from canonical module names where that removes paired module/path literals without changing freshness or cache rules.
    ☑ Extend tests for source-versus-installed host entry paths.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate.

Phase 2 - Extract protocol and support namespaces:
    ☑ Create `src/dwarfspec/protocol/configuration/` and move `error_formats.lua`, `settings.lua`, and `config_schema.lua` as `schema.lua` with their callers and tests.
    ☑ Move the current pure event contract, validation, and journal operations together into `protocol/events.lua`; do not invent a host-side event-validation module.
    ☑ Keep pure shared schema logic in `protocol/schemas.lua`.
    ☑ Create `protocol/diagnostics/` and move focus comparison values and payload validation into `base_screen_focus_comparisons.lua` and `focus.lua`.
    ☑ Move the stable shared focus-warning formatter into `protocol/diagnostics/focus_warning.lua`; leave native focus observation for the host phase.
    ☑ Move cross-runtime enums into `protocol/enums/`: event types, owner kinds, result policies, result states, runner failure kinds, run states, scheduler failure kinds, and test statuses.
    ☑ Leave driver-only enums with the future driver namespace.
    ☑ Create `src/dwarfspec/support/` and the matching `tests/unit/support/` tree.
    ☑ Extract pure project path normalization into `support/project_paths.lua` before moving `result_store.lua`.
    ☑ Move `glob.lua`, `identity_labels.lua`, and `immutable_enum.lua` into `support/` with their callers and tests.
    ☑ Update all callers, dynamic paths, and matching unit specs in the same cohesive moves.
    ☑ Confirm protocol and support modules load without controller, host, driver, `df`, or `dfhack` dependencies.
    ☑ Review the moved modules' `require()` and `loadfile()` dependencies against the protocol and support ownership rules.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate.

Phase 3 - Create the controller namespace:
    ☑ Create the controller directory and matching unit-test subtrees from the proposal's initial Mermaid tree.
    ☑ Move external configuration loading into `controller/configuration/config.lua` and `controller/configuration/dotenv.lua`.
    ☑ Move controller project discovery into `controller/discovery/project.lua` and consume only pure shared path operations from `support/project_paths.lua`.
    ☑ Move `process.lua` and the still-monolithic `runner.lua` into `controller/execution/` without decomposing the runner yet.
    ☑ Move `diagnostic_formatter.lua` and `report.lua` into `controller/reporting/`.
    ☑ Move `result_store.lua` into `controller/` only after removing its dependency on host project state.
    ☑ Keep root `cli.lua` as the existing facade and allow it to import controller, protocol, and support during the initial migration.
    ☑ Update CLI callers, tests, and all matching unit-spec paths atomically.
    ☑ Verify `help`, `list`, `run`, status, abort, recovery, and inspection commands resolve their host entry paths through `layout.lua`.
    ☑ Confirm controller modules do not load host or driver code into the external interpreter.
    ☑ Review controller `require()` and `loadfile()` dependencies and confirm they do not reach host, driver, or DFHack-only modules.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate.

Phase 4 - Create the driver namespace:
    ☑ Create the driver directory and matching unit-test subtrees from the proposal's initial Mermaid tree.
    ☑ Move built-in run-scoped commands into `driver/commands/`.
    ☑ Move input state, mouse button, pointer anchor, pointer space, and pointer adapter modules into `driver/input/`.
    ☑ Move component and mount lifecycle modules into `driver/mount/`, retaining monolithic `mount_context.lua` for later decomposition.
    ☑ Move render observation and instrumentation modules into `driver/render/`.
    ☑ Move subject, native lookup, interaction target, retained-identity, and view adapter modules into `driver/subjects/`.
    ☑ Move driver-only screen-origin and state-change-event modules into `driver/`.
    ☑ Keep root `ds.lua` as the composition root and update its local loader to the new canonical module names.
    ☑ Do not move or redesign host-owned save-game, overlay-registration, scheduling, cleanup, or diagnostic workflows in this phase.
    ☑ Update matching unit specs and all `require()` and `loadfile()` paths with each cohesive production move.
    ☑ Confirm no module beneath `driver/` imports a module beneath `host/` or the old `automation/` namespace.
    ☑ Run focused live coverage for mounting, native screens, pointer input, events, and cleanup.
    ☑ Complete the remaining standard phase gate items.

Phase 5 - Replace automation with the host namespace:
    ☑ Create the host directory and matching unit-test subtrees from the proposal's initial Mermaid tree.
    ☑ Move Busted lifecycle, cleanup, coroutine scheduling, suite identity, output handling, and monolithic host execution into `host/execution/`.
    ☑ Move projects, the monolithic scheduler, service state, and snapshots into `host/service/`.
    ☑ Move extensions, filesystem and system adapters, and the host project loader into `host/environment/`; rename the loader to `project_environment.lua`.
    ☑ Move diagnostics, problem-source classification, and native focus observation into `host/diagnostics/`.
    ☑ Update problem-source classification for the organized source tree without exposing absolute local paths.
    ☑ Move overlay registration and save-game load, mount, and unload workflows into `host/game/`.
    ☑ Move directly invoked scripts into `host/entrypoints/` last, preserving continuous `dfhack-run` invocation.
    ☑ Keep only the tiny duplicated bootstrap needed for each entrypoint to derive the Lua module root; do not duplicate downstream host paths.
    ☑ Update `layout.lua`, controller callers, dynamic loaders, integration helpers, and matching tests with each entrypoint move.
    ☑ Remove the empty `src/dwarfspec/automation/` directory after all references have moved.
    ☑ Confirm `host/execution/host.lua` and root `ds.lua` are the only uses of the documented composition exception.
    ☑ Verify every CLI command path against the relocated host entrypoints.
    ☑ Run focused live coverage for events, save-game commands, cleanup, scheduler recovery, and host execution.
    ☑ Complete the remaining standard phase gate items.

Phase 6 - Close the initial directory-migration milestone:
    ☑ Compare the resulting production tree with the proposal's initial Mermaid tree and resolve every unexplained difference.
    ☑ Confirm `src/ds.d.lua` remains at the source root and contains no stale organized-source paths.
    ☑ Confirm unit specs mirror controller, host, driver, protocol, and support ownership beneath `tests/unit/`.
    ☑ Keep live suites under `tests/automation/`, destructive suites under `tests/destructive/`, and multi-process scenarios under `tests/integration/`.
    ☑ Do not move checked-in protocol fixtures or generated `.test-results` for visual symmetry.
    ☑ Update `docs/architecture.md`, contributor guidance, and other affected documentation with the new boundaries and public-versus-internal contract.
    ☑ Remove stale internal paths from source, tests, scripts, declarations, and documentation.
    ☑ Rerun the Phase 0 source-declaration check and require the valid and invalid fixtures to retain their expected results.
    ☑ Rerun the full-unit, syntax, and dependency-review gates after the source-organization changes.
    ☑ Run every focused live scope recorded in Phase 0 and compare it with the recorded baseline, reporting cleanup separately.
    ☑ Run the full non-destructive live suite and confirm it introduces no failures relative to Phase 0.
    ☑ Run `tools/Run-MultiProjectIntegration.ps1` and confirm it introduces no failures relative to Phase 0.
    ☑ Document any still-present baseline failure without treating it as a regression or silently claiming a clean full pass.
    ☑ Run `git diff --check` and confirm unrelated worktree and index state remains preserved.
    ☑ Declare the initial directory migration complete only after every item in this phase is satisfied.

Phase 7 - Approve the late decomposition designs:
    ☑ Create a focused decomposition proposal for root `ds.lua`.
    ☑ Create a focused decomposition proposal for `host/execution/host.lua`.
    ☑ Create a focused decomposition proposal for `host/service/scheduler.lua`.
    ☑ Create a focused decomposition proposal for `driver/mount/mount_context.lua`.
    ☑ Create a focused decomposition proposal for `controller/execution/runner.lua`.
    ☑ In each proposal, name every extracted module, its responsibility, dependency changes, direct tests, and proposed Mermaid subtree.
    ☑ Reject generic destinations such as `utils.lua`, `helpers.lua`, or `common.lua`.
    ☑ Reconcile the proposals together so they do not assign the same responsibility to multiple modules.
    ☑ Obtain explicit approval for all five proposals before implementing any late decomposition.

Phase 8 - Decompose root ds.lua:
    ☑ Implement only the module boundaries approved in the `ds.lua` decomposition proposal.
    ☑ Retain run-scoped API construction and public command binding in root `ds.lua`.
    ☑ Create `driver/commands/wait.lua`, `game_state.lua`, `view_position.lua`, `mount.lua`, `input.lua`, `pointer.lua`, and `capture.lua` with exactly the approved command-validation and adaptation responsibilities; keep event matching in the existing `await_event.lua`.
    ☑ Create `driver/subjects/native_subject_source.lua` for native/overlay source construction and dual-root resolution.
    ☑ Create `driver/render/command_observer.lua` for command events and bounded mount diagnostics.
    ☑ Preserve the public injected `ds` API and each local loader's cache and freshness behavior.
    ☑ Do not move or redesign host-owned save-game, overlay-registration, or diagnostic workflows; reserve that ownership change for the follow-up plan.
    ☑ Add the approved direct behavioral specs for `wait`, `game_state`, `view_position`, `mount`, `input`, `pointer`, `capture`, `native_subject_source`, and `command_observer`; do not add file-existence tests.
    ☑ Confirm the implementation matches the approved decomposition proposal and its Mermaid subtree.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate and the relevant focused live driver tests.

Phase 9 - Decompose host execution:
    ☑ Implement only the module boundaries approved for `host/execution/host.lua`.
    ☑ Create `host/environment/module_environment.lua` for pinned/project module setup, restoration, eviction, and audit behavior.
    ☑ Create `host/execution/suite_discovery.lua`, `example_lifecycle.lua`, `suite_executor.lua`, `run_assembly.lua`, `run_lifecycle.lua`, and `transport_publication.lua` with exactly the approved responsibilities.
    ☑ Keep `host/execution/host.lua` as the service-facing orchestration facade and documented fresh loader of root `dwarfspec.ds`; inject that factory into `run_assembly.lua`.
    ☑ Preserve transport output, cleanup order, error handling, and module freshness behavior.
    ☑ Add the approved direct behavioral specs for `module_environment`, `suite_discovery`, `example_lifecycle`, `suite_executor`, `run_assembly`, `run_lifecycle`, and `transport_publication`.
    ☑ Confirm the implementation matches the approved decomposition proposal and its Mermaid subtree.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate and focused host-execution live tests.

Phase 10 - Decompose the scheduler:
    ☑ Implement only the module boundaries approved for `host/service/scheduler.lua`.
    ☑ Extract request and authority validation into `host/service/scheduler/request_validation.lua`, including detached selections, request keys, owner kinds, lease settings, result paths, exact run identities, and mutation-free authorization.
    ☑ Extract run admission into `host/service/scheduler/admission.lua`, including identity allocation, project/result-path gates, idempotent retries, queued-record construction, and queued-transition requests.
    ☑ Extract FIFO queue policy into `host/service/scheduler/queue.lua`, including head selection/revalidation, activation or pre-execution rejection, queued cancellation, and queue-lease expiry.
    ☑ Extract lease policy into `host/service/scheduler/leases.lua`, including external renewal, in-process heartbeat, lease-check scheduling, and expired-active abort requests.
    ☑ Extract all scheduler mutations and protocol-event appends into `host/service/scheduler/transitions.lua`, covering queued, activated, running, cleaning, cancellation, terminal release, and quarantine transitions.
    ☑ Extract scheduler recovery policy into `host/service/scheduler/recovery.lua`, including active abort authorization, quarantine verification/clearing, acknowledgement, operator discard, and reservation release.
    ☑ Keep `host/service/scheduler.lua` as the scheduler facade called by `service.lua`, keep the service registry as the one authoritative state object, and route every scheduler mutation and protocol event through `transitions.lua`.
    ☑ Preserve admission, scheduling, cancellation, lease, abort, quarantine, acknowledgement, discard, recovery, and persistence-reservation behavior.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/host/service/scheduler/request_validation_spec.lua` for malformed values, detached copies, idempotency matching, result-path conflicts, owner capabilities, and operator authority.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/host/service/scheduler/admission_spec.lua` for successful admission, collisions, outstanding-run gates, retries, event identity, and mutation only after validation.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/host/service/scheduler/queue_spec.lua` for FIFO ordering, incompatible projects, cancellation, queue expiry, blocked/quarantined executors, and activation handoff.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/host/service/scheduler/leases_spec.lua` for renewal ownership, timer replacement, heartbeat policy, expiry boundaries, and stale-generation rejection.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/host/service/scheduler/transitions_spec.lua` for the legal transition matrix, generation guards, event order, executor release, cleanup-confirmed versus quarantine, and atomic mutation.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/host/service/scheduler/recovery_spec.lua` for normal/operator abort, exact generations, successful and failed clean-state proof, acknowledgement, discard, and reservation release.
    ☑ Confirm the implementation matches the approved decomposition proposal and its Mermaid subtree.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate and focused scheduler recovery live tests.

Phase 11 - Decompose mount context:
    ☑ Implement only the module boundaries approved for `driver/mount/mount_context.lua`.
    ☑ Extract mount allocation and ownership into `driver/mount/mount_resource_ownership.lua`, including mount identities, owned/borrowed construction, the single-current-mount guard, cleanup registration, explicit unmount handoff, and construction rollback.
    ☑ Extract subject registration and resolution into `driver/mount/mount_subject_resolution.lua`, including source registration, adapter refresh, path parsing/traversal, retained subjects, retained identity, and stale/ambiguous rejection.
    ☑ Extract mounted command execution into `driver/mount/mount_command_execution.lua`, including current mount/subject resolution, command invocation, render-aware mutation waits, and enriched command/component diagnostics.
    ☑ Extract teardown and proof into `driver/mount/mount_cleanup_verification.lua`, including subject release, observer/pointer detachment, owned-screen dismissal, mount cleanup, failure aggregation, and exact cleanup-state evidence.
    ☑ Keep `driver/mount/mount_context.lua` as the constructed context facade with its established method surface.
    ☑ Preserve mount lifecycle, teardown order, retained subject identity, diagnostic content, and cleanup confirmation.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/driver/mount/mount_resource_ownership_spec.lua` for owned/native mounts, duplicate mount rejection, identity, cleanup registration, explicit unmount, and construction rollback.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/driver/mount/mount_subject_resolution_spec.lua` for Lua/native/overlay sources, child paths, retained identity, replacement/removal, ambiguity, and bounded available-child diagnostics.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/driver/mount/mount_command_execution_spec.lua` for current-mount guards, command arguments/returns, synchronous and awaited mutations, render failure, timeout, and diagnostic preservation.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/driver/mount/mount_cleanup_verification_spec.lua` for LIFO teardown, borrowed-resource preservation, owned dismissal, pointer/render restoration, multiple failures, idempotence, and verified state.
    ☑ Confirm the implementation matches the approved decomposition proposal and its Mermaid subtree.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate and focused component/native mount live tests.
    ☑ Replace the broad `M.install(context)` service-locator pattern with narrow, explicit dependency contracts for each extracted mount module, including explicit command-to-subject and command-to-render dependencies.
    ☑ Remove the extraction-only `_subject_adapter` method from the mount-context facade and confirm its method surface exactly matches the pre-decomposition surface.
    ☑ Expand `mount_subject_resolution_spec.lua` to exercise actual native and overlay resolution behavior, including their path traversal, retained identity, replacement/removal, and ambiguity contracts instead of only registering differently labeled fake sources.
    ☑ Expand `mount_command_execution_spec.lua` to cover render failure and render timeout as distinct direct behaviors with preserved diagnostics.
    ☑ Correct `mount_cleanup_verification_spec.lua` to model an actual borrowed mounted screen and prove that cleanup preserves it while continuing to dismiss owned screens.
    ☑ Recheck the corrected implementation against the approved Mermaid dependencies, then rerun the standard phase gate and focused component/native mount live tests with cleanup confirmation reported separately.

Phase 12 - Decompose the controller runner:
    ☑ Implement only the module boundaries approved for `controller/execution/runner.lua`.
    ☑ Extract command validation and construction into `controller/execution/command_builder.lua`, including package/entrypoint validation and exact probe, bootstrap, poll, query, acknowledgement, abort, and recovery argument vectors.
    ☑ Extract transport invocation into `controller/execution/transport_client.lua`, including process invocation, connection/process failure classification, and canonical report parsing with expected identities and cursors.
    ☑ Extract run polling into `controller/execution/run_poller.lua`, including queued/active polling, event formatting, lease renewal, cursor advancement, and distinct queue/execution timeout budgets.
    ☑ Extract cancellation and recovery orchestration into `controller/execution/run_recovery.lua`, including queued cancellation, active abort, terminal acknowledgement, timed-out invocation recovery, and explicit executor recovery.
    ☑ Extract result classification and persistence orchestration into `controller/execution/result_interpreter.lua`, including stable result states, invocation-result documents, and calls to the existing result-store policy.
    ☑ Keep `controller/execution/runner.lua` as the public orchestration facade for run, abort, status, history, inspection, logs, and executor recovery.
    ☑ Preserve `process.lua` as the subprocess authority, `report.lua` as the transport/schema authority, and `result_store.lua` as the safe-replacement authority; use `transport_client.lua` only for transport invocation and classification.
    ☑ Preserve command arguments, polling cursors, queue/execution timeouts, cancellation, abort, acknowledgement, recovery, failure kinds, status handling, and result interpretation.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/controller/execution/command_builder_spec.lua` for source/installed paths, every command vector, options, repeated filters, missing dependencies, and unsafe values.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/controller/execution/transport_client_spec.lua` for exit codes, missing/malformed envelopes, identity mismatch, cursor checks, verbose output, and process exceptions.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/controller/execution/run_poller_spec.lua` for activation boundaries, cursor advancement, queue timeout, execution timeout, terminal results, lease renewal, and interruption.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/controller/execution/run_recovery_spec.lua` for queued/active selection, owner capabilities, original-error preservation, cleanup confirmation, acknowledgement failure, and quarantine recovery.
    ☑ Add `tests/unit/controller/execution/result_interpreter_spec.lua` for every failure kind, pre-execution/native outcomes, interrupted/aborted states, persistence policies, write failures, and terminal metadata.
    ☑ Confirm the implementation matches the approved decomposition proposal and its Mermaid subtree.
    ☑ Complete the standard phase gate and focused CLI execution-path tests.
    ☑ Move native terminal-outcome classification, including aborted, cancelled, failed, passed-without-cleanup, and successful outcomes, from `controller/execution/runner.lua` into `controller/execution/result_interpreter.lua` so the facade only sequences the approved services.
    ☑ Restrict `controller/execution/transport_client.lua` to transport invocation, failure classification, and calls to the existing report authority; move verbose runner presentation back to the facade and make event formatting an explicit polling dependency owned by `controller/execution/run_poller.lua` without violating the approved Mermaid dependencies.
    ☑ Expand `tests/unit/controller/execution/command_builder_spec.lua` to verify the complete bootstrap vector and all configured options, plus actual source-layout and installed-layout dependency and entrypoint resolution.
    ☑ Expand `tests/unit/controller/execution/transport_client_spec.lua` to classify process exceptions from ordinary transport, bootstrap, scheduler-status, and retained-run query invocations in addition to probe failures.
    ☑ Expand `tests/unit/controller/execution/run_poller_spec.lua` to prove exact owner-capability lease renewal, cursor and expectation forwarding, one-time activation at the queued-to-active boundary, ordered event formatting/emission, and observation persistence.
    ☑ Expand `tests/unit/controller/execution/run_recovery_spec.lua` to prove queued cancellation versus active recovery selection, owner-capability and identity/cursor forwarding, successful and failed cleanup confirmation, original-error preservation, acknowledgement failure, and quarantine recovery.
    ☑ Expand `tests/unit/controller/execution/result_interpreter_spec.lua` to cover every applicable runner failure kind, native terminal classification, no-file and file persistence policies, write failures, and complete terminal metadata.
    ☑ Recheck the corrected implementation against the approved responsibility table and Mermaid subtree, then rerun the standard phase gate, focused CLI execution-path tests, and focused live execution with cleanup confirmation reported separately.

Phase 13 - Decompose cli.lua near the end:
    ☐ Begin only after Phases 8 through 12 are complete.
    ☐ Move argument parsing, option validation, and help construction into `controller/command_line.lua`.
    ☐ Move command selection and orchestration into `controller/application.lua`.
    ☐ Reduce root `cli.lua` to delegation to `controller.application`.
    ☐ Review the fully decomposed root `cli.lua` and confirm it imports only `controller.application`.
    ☐ Preserve all existing command names, options, validation, help output, exit behavior, and orchestration behavior.
    ☐ Add `tests/unit/controller/command_line_spec.lua` for argument parsing, option validation, help construction, invalid input, and stable parse results.
    ☐ Add `tests/unit/controller/application_spec.lua` for command selection, controller orchestration, exit behavior, and error propagation.
    ☐ Verify `help`, `list`, `run`, status, abort, recovery, and inspection paths.
    ☐ Complete the standard phase gate.

Phase 14 - Close full source organization:
    ☐ Confirm the five proposal-driven decompositions match their approved Mermaid subtrees and the CLI matches its specified two-module split.
    ☐ Confirm the full production tree equals the initial migration tree plus the approved decomposition subtrees and the two specified CLI modules.
    ☐ Confirm all root-facade and namespace dependency rules pass, including both directions of the temporary composition exception.
    ☐ Confirm every extracted responsibility has direct behavioral coverage and no generic dumping-ground module was introduced.
    ☐ Confirm source declarations and documentation contain no stale paths or unsupported public module claims.
    ☐ Rerun the Phase 0 source-declaration check and require the valid and invalid fixtures to retain their expected results.
    ☐ Reuse the Phase 13 full-unit, syntax, and dependency-review results if no source, declaration, test, or tool file changed afterward; otherwise rerun them.
    ☐ Run `git diff --check` after all final source, test, declaration, tool, planning, and documentation changes.
    ☐ Run every focused live scope from Phase 0, confirm it introduces no failures relative to the Phase 0 baseline, and report test results and cleanup evidence separately.
    ☐ Run the full non-destructive live suite and confirm it introduces no failures relative to Phase 0.
    ☐ Run `tools/Run-MultiProjectIntegration.ps1` and confirm it introduces no failures relative to Phase 0.
    ☐ Preserve and report any pre-existing baseline failure distinctly from the completed reorganization evidence.
    ☐ Confirm unrelated worktree and index state remains preserved.
    ☐ Declare full source organization complete only after every item in this phase is satisfied.

Phase 15 - Create and present the option 2 follow-up plan:
    ☐ Create a separate planning document for removing the temporary root `ds.lua` composition exception; do not implement that ownership redesign in this checklist.
    ☐ Inventory every host module still loaded by `ds.lua` after full source organization.
    ☐ Define the narrow host capabilities required by driver-facing code.
    ☐ Evaluate moving run-scoped save-game workflows, overlay registration, and interaction diagnostics under `driver/` according to ownership.
    ☐ Define explicit injected interfaces for host scheduling, cleanup, project-environment, and service capabilities.
    ☐ Preserve fresh `loadfile()` behavior and source-versus-installed loading in the proposed design.
    ☐ Specify boundary tests proving modules beneath `driver/` do not import `host/`.
    ☐ Specify incremental implementation and verification gates without changing the public `ds` API.
    ☐ Present the follow-up plan for approval and record its approval state.
    ☐ Treat approval as a prerequisite for option 2 implementation, not as a prerequisite for completing this source-organization checklist.

Final handoff:
    ☐ Summarize the initial directory migration and each approved late decomposition.
    ☐ Report focused unit, full unit, syntax/formatting, boundary, focused live, full live, multi-process integration, cleanup, declaration-path, documentation, and diff evidence distinctly.
    ☐ List pre-existing baseline failures separately from regressions and confirm no new full-live failures were introduced.
    ☐ Identify the temporary `ds.lua` composition exception as remaining intentional architecture until the separately approved option 2 plan is implemented.
    ☐ Confirm no required checklist item remains incomplete or record the exact blocker and affected items.
